  "guidebook": {
    "intro": "Cooking brings together everything: commands, time words, food and sharing. Luwombo, a stew steamed in banana leaves, is the dish of celebration.",
    "phrases": [
      { "target": "Teeka entamu ku muliro.", "base": "Put the pot on the fire." },
      { "target": "Sooka sala obutungulu.", "base": "First cut the onions." },
      { "target": "Oluvannyuma gattako ennyaanya.", "base": "Then add the tomatoes." },
      { "target": "Tusiba matooke mu ndagala.", "base": "We wrap matooke in banana leaves." },
      { "target": "Emmere eyidde. Tulye!", "base": "The food is ready. Let’s eat!" },
      { "target": "Nkkuse, weebale nnyo.", "base": "I’m full, thank you very much." },
      { "target": "Webale okufumba!", "base": "Thanks for cooking!" }
    ],
    "tips": [
      {
        "id": "recipe-steps",
        "title": "Steps in order",
        "body": [
          "A recipe is a list of commands: **Sala** (cut), **Teeka** (put), **Gattako** (add), **Siika** (fry). Start with **Sooka** (first) and continue with **Oluvannyuma** (then, afterwards).",
          "**-ko** at the end of a verb adds “a bit, some”: **Gattako omunnyo** (add some salt). You met it in **Kendeezaako** (lower it a bit)."
        ]
      }
    ],
    "culture": [
      {
        "title": "Matooke in banana leaves",
        "body": [
          "Matooke is peeled, wrapped in **endagala** (banana leaves), steamed for hours and mashed inside the leaves. The leaves keep it warm and give it its taste, and they are used as plates and lids too."
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Webale okufumba",
        "body": [
          "After a meal, thank the cook: **Webale okufumba**. Saying **Nkkuse** (I’m full) is the polite way to stop, otherwise your plate will be filled again."
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
